Subject: [PATCH] Build the runtime as ARM64EC (wine-mono on Hangover, aarch64 hosts)

An ARM64EC build of libmono: the JIT emits plain ARM64 for managed code and the native half runs as
native ARM64 under wine's ARM64EC personality, so no x64 code of the runtime or of managed code goes
through the x86_64 emulator. Only the game's own x64 DLLs (Havok, steam_api64, ...) still do, behind
per-signature exit/entry thunks that this patch adds to the JIT. Everything here is under
`__arm64ec__`/the `arm64ec-*mingw*` host, so the x86_64 build of the same tree is unchanged.

configure.ac
  * `arm64ec-*mingw*` joins the `aarch64-*mingw*` case: TARGET/HOST ARM64 (the Windows-ARM64 port) with
    an arm64ec-w64-mingw32 toolchain. configure forces CC="$host-gcc" for every cross build, so without
    this an aarch64 host silently compiled plain ARM64 objects that wine refuses to load in an x64
    process. Windows 10 API level for the ARM64 hosts (RtlAddGrowableFunctionTable and friends).

Compiler-macro guards (mini-codegen.c, monobitset.c, valgrind.h, mono-memory-model.h, mono-context.{h,c},
mono-codeman.c): clang's arm64ec target predefines __x86_64__ (not __aarch64__), so every conditional
that means "x86 machine code" must exclude __arm64ec__.

mono-context.c: the exception/suspend CONTEXT under ARM64EC is the x64-layout CONTEXT with the ARM64
registers aliased into it (ARM64EC_NT_CONTEXT); map it to and from MonoContext. mono-context.h:
MONO_WIN32_CONTEXT_PC for the two exceptions-arm64.c sites that read the PC.

mono-mmap-windows.c / mono-codeman.c: JIT memory must be registered in the process's EC code bitmap or
the emulator treats it as x64 bytes: allocate through NtAllocateVirtualMemoryEx with
MEM_EXTENDED_PARAMETER_EC_CODE and never from an executable heap.

mini-arm64.c prolog: the stack probe for frames over 4 KiB calls `__chkstk_arm64ec` — the EC helper, same
x15 = size/16 convention — and only when that address is EC code; the `__chkstk` export is the x64-facing
fast-forward stub, which EC code must never branch to.

mini-arm64.h: ARM64EC reserves x13, x14, x23, x24, x28 and v16-v31 for the emulator (absent from every
captured CONTEXT, not preserved across x64 code); keep them out of the allocator, x27 is args_reg. The
dynamic function tables are built from ARM64_RUNTIME_FUNCTION entries: the unwinder reads an EC
process's growable tables as 8-byte ARM64 entries, and mingw's RUNTIME_FUNCTION for the arm64ec target
is the 12-byte AMD64 layout.

mini-arm64.c, mini.c, cpu-arm64.md: the EC boundary.
  * OUT: a p/invoke whose target is not EC code at JIT time loads x11 = target, x10 = an exit thunk for
    the call's argument/return SHAPE, calls __os_arm64x_check_icall and then the address it returns
    (the target itself, the EC implementation behind an ARM64X fast-forward stub, or the thunk with
    x9 = the x64 target). Exit thunks re-marshal AAPCS64 arguments into the x64 convention: positional
    rcx/rdx/r8/r9 = x0-x3, xmm0-3 = v0-3, the rest on the stack after the 32-byte shadow space,
    aggregates by their REAL size (1/2/4/8 bytes in the integer slot, anything else as a pointer to a
    copy); the call is `blr x16` to __os_arm64x_dispatch_call_no_redirect, as the emulator requires.
  * IN: mono_codegen reserves a prefix in front of every native-to-managed wrapper holding an entry
    thunk and the offset word at entry-4 that the emulator reads when x64 code calls the wrapper. The
    thunk saves q6-q15 (x64 preserves them whole), builds the AAPCS64 layout from x0-x3/v0-v3 and the x64
    stack (x4), calls the wrapper and returns through __os_arm64x_dispatch_ret with the result in x8.
  * Both thunks copy every register source into a frame image first; a two-float HFA at position 0
    occupies s0 AND s1, so no register-to-register order is safe.
  * Thunks live in EC-marked code manager memory and are cached per shape.

exceptions-arm64.c: the growable function tables are registered from ARM64_RUNTIME_FUNCTION entries
(the mini-arm64.h type mapping above; the two RtlAddGrowableFunctionTable calls take the table as a
plain pointer), and the vectored handler and mono_arch_ip_from_context read the PC through
MONO_WIN32_CONTEXT_PC.

Not handled: varargs p/invoke signatures (a trap with a warning at thunk creation).

Suspend policy: the build is configured with --enable-cooperative-suspend. Under the ARM64EC contract a
thread executing x64 code is not stopped by SuspendThread: the emulator suspends it at its next doorbell
poll and GetThreadContext waits for that, so a preemptive (or hybrid) stop-the-world is safe only if the
emulator polls inside every loop. Either removes the deadlock: cooperative suspend (threads stop at the
runtime's own safepoints; SuspendThread is never called on a running thread) or an emulator that polls
on conditional back-edges (propnix emulators/fex/patches/0003). This build takes the first and keeps the
policy runtime-selectable (MONO_THREADS_SUSPEND). configure defaults hybrid on only for X86|AMD64 hosts,
so the flag is explicit. Investigation and measurements: docs/research/mono-arm64ec.md.

diff --git a/configure.ac b/configure.ac
index cc8c5ea93..e8314ad19 100644
--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -190,6 +190,14 @@ case "$host" in
 
 		# Windows 7 or later is required
 		WIN32_CPPFLAGS="-DWINVER=0x0601 -D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0601 -D_WIN32_IE=0x0501 -D_UNICODE -DUNICODE -DWIN32_THREADS -DFD_SETSIZE=1024"
+		case "$host" in
+			aarch64-*mingw*|arm64ec-*mingw*)
+				# Windows on ARM64 is Windows 10 or later; the ARM64 port's growable function tables
+				# (RtlAddGrowableFunctionTable, Windows 8) and ARM64EC code allocation (VirtualAlloc2,
+				# Windows 10 RS4) are declared only at that API level.
+				WIN32_CPPFLAGS="-DWINVER=0x0A00 -D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0A00 -DNTDDI_VERSION=0x0A000005 -D_WIN32_IE=0x0501 -D_UNICODE -DUNICODE -DWIN32_THREADS -DFD_SETSIZE=1024"
+				;;
+		esac
 		CPPFLAGS="$CPPFLAGS $WIN32_CPPFLAGS"
 		WIN32_LDFLAGS="-lbcrypt -lmswsock -lws2_32 -lole32 -loleaut32 -lpsapi -lversion -ladvapi32 -lwinmm -lkernel32 -lntdll -liphlpapi"
 		LDFLAGS="$LDFLAGS $WIN32_LDFLAGS"
@@ -4307,7 +4315,7 @@ case "$host" in
 		BTLS_SUPPORTED=no
 		BTLS_PLATFORM=arm
 		;;
-	aarch64-*mingw*)
+	aarch64-*mingw*|arm64ec-*mingw*)
 		TARGET=ARM64;
 		arch_target=arm64;
 		BTLS_SUPPORTED=no
